    /// Answered to the wallet the deposit belongs to. The deposit_id was the whole authorisation once, and is not any more: an id leaks — into a log, a crash report, an analytics event — and it can also be asked for, since recording the same transaction again returns the deposit that already exists, id included. A deposit of another wallet answers 404 rather than 403, because telling those apart would confirm that the id exists.
    /// Everything in this response stays public by construction all the same — the amounts are in the Ethereum transaction and the account index follows from the address — and it must stay that way. Anything private added here would be published to whoever watches the chain.
    /// account_index is answered live for as long as the record has none, since that is the field a client is waiting on. The credit is not: it comes from this service's own record, written by the deposit's next scheduled check, so polling faster than that schedule learns nothing about the amount. Once it reports anything but pending the answer no longer changes, so there is nothing to gain by asking again.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `GET /funding/deposit/{deposit_id}`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//funding/deposit/{deposit_id}/get(getDeposit)`.
    func getDeposit(_ input: Operations.getDeposit.Input) async throws -> Operations.getDeposit.Output
    /// Quote a deposit from another asset
    ///
    /// Prices a top-up of the caller's Lighter account paid with an asset on another chain, and returns what the client has to sign to make it happen. Nothing moves here: the quote is an offer, and the payloads are the client's to sign and broadcast from its own wallet.
    /// The money always lands as USDC on Ethereum, because that is where Lighter's gateway is, and the deposit into the gateway is part of the same cross-chain action: the aggregator delivers the USDC and calls the gateway with it, naming destination_address as the account to credit. The client therefore signs only on the source chain.
    /// The pricing is swaps-backend's (`POST /v2/crosschain/quotes` with `destination_target: lighter`): it asks the aggregator, sizes the deposit into the budget and checks the payloads against the quote. This service checks what only it knows -- whose account the deposit credits -- and hands the rest on.
    /// source_amount is a budget, not a price: the payloads returned never ask for more than it, and the deposit is sized to what that amount buys after the aggregator's fees, which is why output.amount and output.min_amount are equal -- the deposit is exact, and the slippage the quote allowed for was on the source side. The whole of source_amount is rarely spent to the last unit.
    /// A source asset that is already USDC on Ethereum needs no aggregator: the payloads are then the approval and the gateway call for the client's own Ethereum key, provider is `direct`, and there is nothing to track.
    /// Native USDC on Arbitrum, Base or Polygon goes through Lighter's bridge (fun.xyz) instead, provider `funxyz`: the one payload is a plain `transfer` of the whole source_amount to the destination's deposit address on that chain, which the bridge credits to the perps account as it is -- no fee, no swap, output.amount equal to source_amount. The address is the destination's own and the same on every deposit. tracking follows it as any other, by matching the transfer the bridge sees there: same chain, same amount, after the quote. The bridge takes no less than 5 USDC for an account it creates and 3 USDC for one that exists; a smaller amount, a key to register (change_pub_key on an account Lighter does not know), or a bridge that does not answer is quoted through the aggregator as before.
    /// USDC on Ethereum goes through the bridge too, but only to top up an account Lighter already knows, with at least 5 USDC: one transfer instead of the approval and the gateway call. The deposit that creates the account, a smaller amount, or an account Lighter did not answer for stay `direct`, as does any quote when the bridge does not answer.
    /// What to sign comes back as one operation: an id, the kind of operation it is, and the payloads in signing order. Each payload says its type and its chain, and the pair decides which meta it carries -- `main` on ton/mainnet is a TonConnect message, `main` on an EVM chain a transaction. A token source puts the approval first, as a payload of its own.
    /// A Bitcoin source is quoted only from an address that already holds source_amount: the aggregator builds the transaction out of the sender's own outputs, so it reads the balance before it prices. An address with too little, or none, answers 400 with reason insufficient_balance -- more funds or a smaller amount fixes it, a retry does not. Other sources are priced without looking at the balance, which the wallet checks when it signs.
    /// A source_amount that buys less than the 1 USDC the gateway accepts, once the aggregator's fees and the destination gas are paid, is 400 with details.reason amount_too_low. The floor moves with those fees -- on a cross-chain route it is mostly Ethereum gas -- so details says it where it can, both in US dollars as a decimal string with two places (e.g. "2.53"):
    /// - details.min_usd_amount: the smallest source_amount quoted now, valued in dollars, rounded up,
    ///   with headroom for the price and the gas to move within a quote's life -- that much of
    ///   source_asset, at the client's own rate, is quoted when asked again. Raised by registering a
    ///   key (change_pub_key), whose call costs gas too. A source that is already USDC on Ethereum
    ///   has the gateway's own floor, "1.00".
    /// - details.expected_amount: what source_amount would have deposited, in dollars, rounded down;
    ///   "0.00" when it does not even cover the fixed costs.
    ///
    /// Either is left out when it could not be priced -- the aggregator refused the amount and then did not answer for the minimum -- and the reason stands without it.
    /// The quote is good for the seconds expires_at says. The aggregator revalidates at fill time, so a stale one is refunded rather than filled at a worse price -- ask again instead of signing an old one.
    /// The wallet's L1 address is destination_address, and the two must agree once the wallet has proved one: a quote for somebody else's account is refused. A wallet that has proved nothing yet -- the first deposit is what creates the account -- is quoted for the address it names, and binds it with POST /account/bind before it records the deposit.
    /// The deposit can also register the account's signing key in the same action, change_pub_key -- including the first deposit, the one that creates the account. While such a deposit is on its way -- its quote named the caller's own account for an address Lighter knew no account of, and the client recorded it with POST /funding/deposit -- every other deposit to that address is refused with 409 account_creation_pending, whatever it pays with, until that one closes: credited, failed, refunded or expired, as GET /funding/deposit/{deposit_id} reports it. A quote that was never recorded holds nothing. Whether a quote with change_pub_key creates the account is asked of Lighter; when it does not answer, that quote is 503 and worth retrying, rather than guessed at. Every other quote asks too, for account_uninitialized only: alongside the aggregator rather than before it, and without waiting -- the flag is left out when Lighter fails or answers late, and the quote is not held up for it. Neither asks when the account is already known -- the wallet's binding names it, or change_pub_key named it by index -- and the flag is then false.
    /// A deposit the aggregator fills that registers the key takes two signatures of the owner's, and what they are over -- the owner's nonces, a deadline -- is this service's to read. So it is two calls. The first carries change_pub_key and no `operation`: the answer is a preview -- `preview: true` -- priced as the deposit will be, with no `tracking`, whose `operation` is the signatures to make -- an `eip7702/authorization` payload and an `eip7821/hermes/delegate` payload (EIP-712 typed data), with the owner's own values in them. The client shows the preview, signs both with the key behind destination_address, and asks again with the same request plus the signed operation in `operation`, in the shared Response Signed Payloads format: the preview's operation id, and per payload its type with `/signed`, its chain_id, version and id as `payload_id`, and `meta.data` -- {y_parity, r, s} for the authorization, the 65-byte signature for the Execute. Nothing else is echoed: the nonces are read from the chain again, and the signatures have to recover to destination_address over them. Return each payload_id exactly as given. That answer is the quote to send: re-priced, since the aggregator's quote is bound to the signed calls and is a new one, and within source_amount as always. The preview's deadline leaves half an hour for the second call.
    /// A source on Ethereum (eth/mainnet, any asset but USDC) is the one exception: there is no fill. The aggregator swaps in the sender's own transaction and runs the deposit and the call into the owner's address from inside it, so that transaction is what delegates the address. The preview's operation is then the `eip7821/hermes/delegate` payload alone and comes back signed alone; the quote to send delegates in its payment -- a token's approval and payment folded into one EIP-7702 batch with its own `meta.authorization`, or a single payment, ETH's or a token's whose allowance is there already, carrying `meta.authorization` for the wallet to sign into it as a type-4 transaction. sender_address must be destination_address there, or it is 400 change_pub_key_sender_not_destination.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `POST /funding/quote`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//funding/quote/post(quoteDeposit)`.
    func quoteDeposit(_ input: Operations.quoteDeposit.Input) async throws -> Operations.quoteDeposit.Output
    /// Quote a withdrawal into another asset
    ///
    /// Prices taking USDC out of the caller's Lighter account and delivering it to a wallet as another asset, on another chain, and returns what the client has to sign. Nothing moves here: the quote is an offer.
    /// Nothing leaves Lighter to L1 either, so there is no withdrawal delay, no claim and no gas. The aggregator takes Lighter itself as the origin: the one payload is a Lighter transfer of the account's USDC collateral into the aggregator's own account on Lighter, and the aggregator pays out destination_asset to recipient_address on its chain. The account is the one the wallet's proved L1 address owns, which the request does not name.
    /// The client signs the transfer as a Lighter transfer (tx type 12) from its account, with the account's API key and -- the receiving account being another owner's -- the owner's L1 key over Lighter's transfer message, and sends it through POST /transactions/send. The fill is followed by tracking.execution_id on GET /funding/status/{execution_id}, as a deposit's is.
    /// amount is a budget, as a deposit's source_amount is: the transfer and Lighter's fee on it never come to more, and what the budget buys after both and the aggregator's fees is output.amount, with output.min_amount the floor under it.
    /// A wallet whose address owns no Lighter account answers 409 no_lighter_account. An amount too small to transfer once the fees are paid answers 400 amount_too_low; a destination asset that is not quoted, unsupported_destination_asset; a recipient that is not an address on its chain, invalid_recipient_address.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `POST /funding/withdraw/quote`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//funding/withdraw/quote/post(quoteWithdraw)`.
    func quoteWithdraw(_ input: Operations.quoteWithdraw.Input) async throws -> Operations.quoteWithdraw.Output
    /// Follow a quoted deposit through the aggregator
    ///
    /// Reports where a cross-chain deposit is, by the execution_id its quote returned: whether the aggregator has seen the source transaction, filled on Ethereum, failed, or refunded. The execution lives at swaps-backend, which follows the fill by the id the aggregator quoted it under, so nothing has to be submitted there first: the wallet broadcasts on its own and polls.
    /// This is how a client that paid from another chain learns the Ethereum transaction hash: the aggregator's fill is the transaction that called the gateway, so once status is success the first entry of tx_hashes. POST /funding/deposit does not need it: a deposit recorded by execution_id is followed through this same execution server-side.
    /// Poll while status is pending. success, failure and refund are final. unknown means the aggregator has no such execution, which is also what an expired, never-broadcast quote looks like.
    /// A `funxyz` quote's execution is followed at Lighter's bridge: pending until the bridge has seen the transfer and credited it, then success. There is no fill on Ethereum, so no tx_hashes; the transfer is in in_tx_hashes once seen. A transfer the bridge has not seen 30 minutes after the quote is unknown, and so is an id no quote handed out.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `GET /funding/status/{execution_id}`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//funding/status/{execution_id}/get(getFundingStatus)`.
    func getFundingStatus(_ input: Operations.getFundingStatus.Input) async throws -> Operations.getFundingStatus.Output
}

/// Convenience overloads for operation inputs.
extension APIProtocol {
    /// Get the caller's Lighter main account
    ///
    /// Resolves the caller's Lighter main account by the L1 (Ethereum) address bound to the wallet and returns a snapshot: account index, balances, and whether an L2 signing key is registered. The two are independent: status is Lighter's own account status, while has_l2_key is read from the account's API keys. One user maps to exactly one Lighter main account.
    /// has_ro_token is not Lighter's answer but ours: it says whether this service holds a usable read-only token for the account, which is what every private read here runs on.
    /// This is also what a client polls while it waits for an account to exist: the index is resolved against Lighter live for as long as the binding has none, so not_registered turns into an account as soon as there is one. A wallet with no binding at all is answered not_registered too, and stays so until POST /account/bind records one -- a deposit made outside this service tells us nothing by itself.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `GET /account`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//account/get(getAccount)`.
    public func getAccount(headers: Operations.getAccount.Input.Headers = .init()) async throws -> Operations.getAccount.Output {
        try await getAccount(Operations.getAccount.Input(headers: headers))
    }
    /// Bind the caller's wallet to its L1 address
    ///
    /// Records which L1 (Ethereum) address a wallet owns, and the Lighter account that address holds when there is one. It is the only endpoint whose whole job is that binding; everywhere else it is a side effect of a write that needed it anyway.
    /// A caller whose Lighter account was funded outside this service has no other way in. Every read here answers from the binding, so until one exists its account is invisible to us, and so is every event Lighter reports about it. A caller about to make its first deposit calls this first: POST /funding/deposit records only against a bound address and does not bind. Calling it again later records the account index once there is one.
    /// The address is never taken from the caller. It is recovered from the signature, and afterwards read back from what that recovered, so deadline and signature are read only while the wallet has proved nothing. Send them anyway: whether they are needed depends on what this service has recorded, which the caller cannot know, and by the time a refusal came back the vault would be locked again. This is what keeps the passcode prompt to once per wallet.
    /// The account index is Lighter's to say, and it is asked for on every call. A wallet whose account does not exist yet is bound to its address alone and answered not_registered, which is not a failure but where every wallet starts: poll GET /account and continue as soon as account_index is set. It is filled in by whichever write learns it first, this one included.
    /// Not knowing is not the same as not having, so when Lighter cannot say what the address owns the request is refused with 503 and nothing is recorded -- a not_registered on a timeout would report a state this service never established. The reads answer the same way. A retry reuses the same signature, so the refusal costs no second prompt.
    /// A 503 says nothing was learned, never that something was undone. The binding is written before the account is read, so a refusal can arrive with the row already recorded -- and the answer is to call again rather than to report a failed registration. The repeat is free: the address is read back from the row, so no signature is needed for it, and re-binding what is recorded writes nothing.
    /// The answer names l1_address either way, and that is worth reading: the address was recovered from a signature rather than sent, so a wallet bound to one it did not expect would otherwise find out only when a later proof, signed over the address it believes in, is refused.
    /// A binding never moves, and there is nothing here with which to redirect it: a bound wallet is served the address it recorded whatever it signs now, so re-binding is accepted and changes nothing, which is the ordinary case.
    /// A 409 therefore reports a collision with another wallet rather than a caller changing its mind: l1_address_taken when that address is already held elsewhere, lighter_account_taken when the account behind it is. The usual cause is one wallet re-imported under a new wallet_id, while the old id still holds the row; somebody has to remove it, which is not something this endpoint can do. wallet_already_bound is the third and is all but unreachable -- it needs two first binds for one wallet to race each other, or Lighter to name a different account for an address it has already placed.
    /// A proof made over some other address is not a conflict but a refused proof, 401: it was signed for a statement this service is not making. Read l1_address in the answer above, or from GET /account, to see which address that is.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `POST /account/bind`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//account/bind/post(bindAccount)`.
    public func bindAccount(
        headers: Operations.bindAccount.Input.Headers = .init(),
        body: Operations.bindAccount.Input.Body
    ) async throws -> Operations.bindAccount.Output {
        try await bindAccount(Operations.bindAccount.Input(
            headers: headers,
            body: body
        ))
    }
    /// Report transactions the caller submitted to Lighter
    ///
    /// Records transactions the caller has already submitted to Lighter so they can be forwarded to the Argus event stream. This service neither signs nor sends them: the client signs locally, submits to Lighter directly, and reports the result here.
    /// Only transactions Lighter accepted belong in the body — one that never reached the venue did not happen and must not be reported.
    /// Report everything the action took, in submission order. Types the event stream does not consume yet are accepted and ignored, so the caller never has to filter: today only ORDER_CREATE is forwarded, and a leverage change reported alongside it is simply dropped.
    /// A 202 means recorded, not delivered: forwarding is asynchronous.
    /// Repeating a report is safe but not free. A repeat that arrives before the event has been forwarded is dropped; one that arrives after produces a second event carrying the same event_id. Delivery is therefore at-least-once and consumers must deduplicate on event_id, which is the transaction hash.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `POST /transactions`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//transactions/post(reportTransactions)`.
    public func reportTransactions(
        headers: Operations.reportTransactions.Input.Headers = .init(),
        body: Operations.reportTransactions.Input.Body
    ) async throws -> Operations.reportTransactions.Output {
        try await reportTransactions(Operations.reportTransactions.Input(
            headers: headers,
            body: body
        ))
    }
    /// Relay signed transactions to Lighter
    ///
    /// Submits transactions the client has already signed. This service does not sign: the trading key never leaves the device, and the signature inside each payload is the whole authorization Lighter checks. What is added here is that the payload has to name the account the caller's proved address owns, and that an accepted order is reported to the event stream without the client having to report it.
    /// One request, one submission, in order. One transaction goes to Lighter's single endpoint and several to its batch one, which does not accept every type — a leverage or margin change has to be the only entry in the request, and Lighter says so itself if it is not.
    /// What may be sent is Lighter's to decide, not this service's: nothing is filtered by type here. A refusal comes back as details.reason in this API's own vocabulary, never as the venue's code.
    /// Accepted means accepted by Lighter, not executed. An order can still be cancelled at matching, by slippage or by its own time in force, and that outcome arrives over the event stream.
    /// Not idempotent. A repeat carries the same nonce and is refused by Lighter, so a retry has to be signed again against a fresh nonce.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `POST /transactions/send`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//transactions/send/post(sendTransactions)`.
    public func sendTransactions(
        headers: Operations.sendTransactions.Input.Headers = .init(),
        body: Operations.sendTransactions.Input.Body
    ) async throws -> Operations.sendTransactions.Output {
        try await sendTransactions(Operations.sendTransactions.Input(
            headers: headers,
            body: body
        ))
    }
    /// The nonce the caller's next Lighter transaction must carry
    ///
    /// Lighter counts transactions per API key slot, and a signed transaction names the nonce it was signed for. Reading one consumes nothing: the same slot answers the same number until a transaction of its own is accepted.
    /// The account comes back with it because the signed payload has to name that too, so signing needs no second call.
    /// Slots are independent counters. Signing in parallel takes one slot per stream — two transactions signed for the same nonce cannot both be accepted.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `GET /transactions/next-nonce`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//transactions/next-nonce/get(getNextNonce)`.
    public func getNextNonce(
        query: Operations.getNextNonce.Input.Query,
        headers: Operations.getNextNonce.Input.Headers = .init()
    ) async throws -> Operations.getNextNonce.Output {
        try await getNextNonce(Operations.getNextNonce.Input(
            query: query,
            headers: headers
        ))
    }
    /// Partner-attribution values the caller signs into its Lighter transactions
    ///
    /// What the client copies, unchanged, into the transactions it signs. Two of them consume these values: an order takes integrator_account_index and the two fees, while ApproveIntegrator -- signed once, before the first order that carries a fee -- takes the account index and the four maximums, with an expiry of now plus approval_ttl_ms.
    /// Fees are millionths of a fill's notional, Lighter's own unit, so 900 is 9 basis points. A zero account index means attribution is off and the client signs no integrator fields at all; that is also the answer while this service has no integrator account configured.
    /// Answered per wallet, and meant to be read before signing rather than kept for the session: the rate is this service's to set and may one day differ between callers.
    /// The account depends on the app. It is read from the User-Agent, the OS in its brackets: "Keeper/26.10.2 (iOS; 27.0; iPhone)" is iOS, "Tonkeeper/26.10.1 (Android; 16; Pixel 9)" is Android, anything else is the third account. An app without its own account gets the third one too. POST /transactions/send checks orders against the same account, so both calls must carry the same User-Agent.
    /// Spot maximums are always zero. This service trades perpetuals, and approving a spot maximum would grant a permission nothing here uses.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `GET /integrator`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//integrator/get(getIntegrator)`.
    public func getIntegrator(headers: Operations.getIntegrator.Input.Headers = .init()) async throws -> Operations.getIntegrator.Output {
        try await getIntegrator(Operations.getIntegrator.Input(headers: headers))
    }
    /// Store the caller's Lighter read-only token (deprecated)
    ///
    /// Deprecated. Use POST /account/ro-token/issue, which mints the token here instead of taking one the client minted. Kept until clients have moved; it is not otherwise being changed.
    /// Two things are worse on this path, and neither can be fixed while the token arrives ready-made. A token minted at Lighter and not submitted here is a live read-only credential nobody knows about, and the two calls it takes can always come apart. And Lighter names a token by an id this endpoint never sees, so nothing stored through it can ever be revoked -- superseding it marks it unused here while it stays alive at the venue.
    /// Persists the caller's Lighter read-only token, encrypted at rest via Vault Transit, and binds the wallet to the Lighter account that token belongs to. The token value travels in the X-Lighter-Auth header; the body carries a signature proving the caller holds the L1 key the account is registered under.
    /// The token is also tried against Lighter before it is stored: one it does not honour for that account is refused here rather than kept and discovered later, when only the background reconciliation would notice.
    /// The account index is not taken from the caller. It is read out of the token and accepted only when it is the account this wallet's L1 address owns, which Lighter is the one to say. That address comes from what the wallet proved earlier -- POST /account/bind, usually -- so a signature is needed here only when it has proved nothing yet. Once bound, a wallet keeps its address and account: a proof naming another, or one another wallet holds, answers 409.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `POST /account/ro-token`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//account/ro-token/post(saveRoToken)`.
    @available(*, deprecated)
    public func saveRoToken(
        headers: Operations.saveRoToken.Input.Headers = .init(),
        body: Operations.saveRoToken.Input.Body
    ) async throws -> Operations.saveRoToken.Output {
        try await saveRoToken(Operations.saveRoToken.Input(
            headers: headers,
            body: body
        ))
    }
    /// Mint the caller's Lighter read-only token and store it
    ///
    /// Mints a read-only token at Lighter for the caller's account, stores it encrypted at rest via Vault Transit, and returns it. It replaces the two steps a client used to take -- minting at Lighter itself, then registering the result here -- with one, so a token cannot be minted and then never registered.
    /// What the caller supplies is lighter_auth: the short-lived credential its Lighter L2 key produced locally. It is a bearer credential and not a signature over this request, so it authorises anything Lighter accepts it for until it expires, and it is passed straight through without being stored or logged. The L2 private key itself never travels.
    /// The account is not named by the caller. It is the one the address bound with POST /account/bind owns, which is also what the token is minted against. This call does not bind: a wallet that has bound no address is answered 409 address_not_bound. A wallet with no Lighter account yet -- one whose first deposit has not been credited -- has nothing to mint for and is answered 409 no_lighter_account.
    /// No signature and no operation proof. lighter_auth is what authorises the mint. The account it names is checked against the bound one before Lighter is asked: another account answers 409 account_mismatch, and a value that is not a Lighter auth token answers 400 invalid_lighter_auth.
    /// The token's expiry and its reach are this service's to set, not the caller's, and are therefore absent from the body: it is minted long-lived, because the private reads this service makes on the caller's behalf run on it and nothing renews it, and scoped to the main account, because a wallet maps to exactly one.
    /// Minting supersedes whatever token the account had. Superseded tokens are revoked at Lighter on a best-effort basis, in this request and only here: revoking takes the same short-lived authorization the mint did, so once this request ends nothing can revoke them any more.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `POST /account/ro-token/issue`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//account/ro-token/issue/post(issueRoToken)`.
    public func issueRoToken(
        headers: Operations.issueRoToken.Input.Headers = .init(),
        body: Operations.issueRoToken.Input.Body
    ) async throws -> Operations.issueRoToken.Output {
        try await issueRoToken(Operations.issueRoToken.Input(
            headers: headers,
            body: body
        ))
    }
    /// The caller's Lighter account tier
    ///
    /// Read live from Lighter, never stored here, so a switch made outside this service shows up too. Not part of GET /account, which clients poll: every read is one more Lighter call.
    /// Lighter answers the tier only with a read-only token, and this service uses the one it stored for the wallet. A wallet with no usable token is answered 400 readonly_token_unavailable: issue one with POST /account/ro-token/issue. A wallet with no Lighter account yet is answered 409 no_lighter_account.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `GET /account/tier`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//account/tier/get(getAccountTier)`.
    public func getAccountTier(headers: Operations.getAccountTier.Input.Headers = .init()) async throws -> Operations.getAccountTier.Output {
        try await getAccountTier(Operations.getAccountTier.Input(headers: headers))
    }
    /// Switch the caller's Lighter account to another tier
    ///
    /// Relays Lighter's changeAccountTier for the caller's account. The tier sets the account's fees, latency and rate limits; Lighter's own docs list them (standard, premium, plus).
    /// What authorises the change is lighter_auth: the short-lived credential the caller's L2 key produced locally. It is passed straight through and never stored or logged. The account is not named by the caller: it is the one the wallet's proved address owns, so a wallet with no Lighter account yet is answered 409.
    /// Which tiers exist and when a switch is allowed is Lighter's to decide, not this service's: new_tier is not checked here. Lighter allows a downgrade once every 24 hours, and a refusal comes back as 400 tier_change_refused.
    ///
    /// - Remark: HTTP `POST /account/tier`.
    /// - Remark: Generated from `#/paths//account/tier/post(changeAccountTier)`.
    public func changeAccountTier(
        headers: Operations.changeAccountTier.Input.Headers = .init(),
        body: Operations.changeAccountTier.Input.Body
    ) async throws -> Operations.changeAccountTier.Output {
        try await changeAccountTier(Operations.changeAccountTier.Input(
            headers: headers,
            body: body
        ))
    }
    /// The caller's open positions
    ///
    /// Every position the wallet currently holds, one per venue and market, with the numbers a position card renders. Only open ones: Lighter keeps a row for every market an account has ever traded, and on a long-lived account the closed rows outnumber the open ones many times over, so a zero size is filtered here rather than on the client.
